%S A400561 2,6,14,30,60,112,196,324,510,770,1122,1586,2184,2940,3880,5032,6426,
%T A400561 8094,10070,12390,15092,18216,21804,25900,30550,35802,41706,48314,
%U A400561 55680,63860,72912,82896,93874,105910,119070,133422,149036,165984,184340,204180,225582,248626
%N A400561 a(n) is the maximum numbers of regions (both closed or open) outside a strictly convex n-polygon, delimited by the n edges and the n*(n-3)/2 diagonals of the polygon itself, where all these elements (edges and diagonals) are extended to infinity as straight lines.
%C A400561 a(n) represents the maximum number of regions. The conditions to have the maximum are: (a) any couple of straight lines defined by edges and/or diagonals are not parallel; (b) any triple of straight lines defined as above, do not intersect in a single common point. Such a polygon could be defined as a totally irregular polygon. The given general formula does not apply for n<=3 (triangle has no diagonals, so the term binomial(3,4) is not defined). Also for n=2 (degenerate polygon) the term binomial(2,4) is not defined, but correctly n=2. Being the total number of lines equal to edges + diagonals = n+n*(n-3)/2, 2*n+n*(n-3) regions are unbounded open regions.
%C A400561 Furthermore, it should be noted that for n>=4 a(n) represents the maximum number of external regions of a convex polygon. However, polygons with different degrees of regularity have a number of external regions <= a(n). For example: the square, the rhombus and the rectangle have n=4 but the number of external regions equal to 12 (<14); the trapezoid has n=4 but the number of external regions equal to 13. Therefore a(n) indicates the maximum number of external regions of a convex polygon in the case where the polygon in question is "totally irregular".
%H A400561 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400561/b400561.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 2..10000</a>
%F A400561 a(n) = 2*binomial(n,2) + 2*binomial(n,4).
%e A400561 For n=3 a(3)=6; for n=4 a(4)=14; for n=5 a(5)=30. It should be noted that for n=2 (two points which define a single line) a(2)=2.
%t A400561 A400561[n_] := 2*(Binomial[n, 2] + Binomial[n, 4]);
%t A400561 Array[A400561, 50, 2] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 11 2026 *)
%K A400561 nonn,easy,new
%O A400561 2,1
